 🐰 What is RabbitMQ?
RabbitMQ is a message broker that allows microservices to communicate asynchronously using the AMQP protocol (Advanced Message Queuing Protocol).

It acts as a middleman between producers (senders) and consumers (receivers).

In simple words:
👉 Producer never talks to Consumer directly.
Producer → Exchange → Queue → Consumer


🧱 Core Concepts
| Term            | Description                                                              |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| **Producer**    | Sends messages to an exchange                                            |
| **Consumer**    | Receives messages from a queue                                           |
| **Queue**       | Stores messages until they are **acked** (or expire / go to DLQ)         |
| **Exchange**    | Routes messages to queues based on rules                                 |
| **Routing Key** | Label used by Direct/Topic exchanges to decide routing                   |
| **Binding**     | Link between exchange and queue (routing key and/or header arguments)    |
| **Message**     | Actual data being passed (e.g., JSON, text, binary)                      |
| **Connection**  | TCP connection from app to RabbitMQ                                      |
| **Channel**     | Lightweight session over a connection (apps publish/consume on channels) |


🔁 Message Flow

Producer → Exchange → Queue(s) → Consumer(s)

Mental model:
```text
Producer
   ↓  (routing key / headers)
Exchange  ----binding---->  Queue  ---->  Consumer
                              ↑
                     stores until ACK
```

Default exchange tip:
RabbitMQ has a nameless **default direct exchange**.
If routing key = queue name, message goes “directly” to that queue.


🧪 Exchange Types in RabbitMQ
RabbitMQ uses exchanges to route messages to queues based on routing rules. Here are the 4 types:

1. 🔵 Direct Exchange
Routes messages to queues based on an **exact** routing key match.

Example:
Routing Key = "user.created"
Queue bound with "user.created" receives the message.

Use Case:
Send a message to a specific service (e.g., user events to the UserService).

2. 🟣 Topic Exchange
Routes messages based on **pattern matching** in routing keys.

Uses wildcards (words separated by dots `.`):
* → matches exactly one word
# → matches zero or more words

Example:
Routing Key = "order.placed.us"

Queue with binding "order.*.us" will receive it.
Queue with binding "order.#" will also receive it.

Use Case:
Complex routing (e.g., events by region, service, or type).

3. 🟡 Fanout Exchange
Routes messages to **all** bound queues, ignores routing key.

Example:
Broadcast message to multiple services: Email, SMS, Logging.

Use Case:
Broadcasting notifications or events (e.g., OrderPlaced goes to multiple consumers).

4. 🟤 Headers Exchange
Uses message **headers** instead of routing keys for routing.

Example:
Header: {"env": "prod", "type": "alert"}

Queue binding: match if header `env = prod` AND `type = alert`
(`x-match=all` → all headers must match; `x-match=any` → any one can match)

Use Case:
Complex or dynamic routing using metadata in headers.

✅ Summary Table of Exchange Types
| Exchange Type | Routing Logic                    | Use Case                              |
| ------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| **Direct**    | Exact routing key match          | Simple service-to-service messaging   |
| **Topic**     | Pattern match with wildcards     | Multi-tenant or feature-based routing |
| **Fanout**    | Broadcast to all bound queues    | Notifications, pub-sub broadcasts     |
| **Headers**   | Based on message header matching | Dynamic, metadata-based routing       |


👥 Competing Consumers vs Pub/Sub (VERY IMPORTANT)

Competing consumers (load sharing):
Multiple consumers listen to the **same queue**.
Each message is delivered to **only one** consumer.

```text
Queue: sms-jobs
  ├── Consumer1  (gets msg A)
  ├── Consumer2  (gets msg B)
  └── Consumer3  (gets msg C)
```

Pub/Sub / fan-out (every service gets a copy):
Bind **multiple queues** to Fanout/Topic.
Each queue (service) receives its own copy.

```text
Fanout Exchange: order.placed
  ├── email-queue   → Email Service
  ├── sms-queue     → SMS Service
  └── log-queue     → Logging Service
```


⚖️ Prefetch (QoS) — Fair Dispatch
Prefetch limits how many **unacked** messages a consumer can hold at once.

Why it matters:
Without prefetch, one fast connection can get flooded / one slow consumer can hoard work.
With prefetch=1 (common for heavy jobs), RabbitMQ gives a new message only after ACK → fairer distribution.


✅ Ack / Nack (Delivery Control)
| Action | Meaning |
| ------ | ------- |
| **Ack** | Message processed successfully → remove from queue |
| **Nack / Reject + requeue** | Failed → put back for retry |
| **Nack / Reject + no requeue** | Failed → discard or send to DLQ |

Auto-ack: message removed as soon as delivered (risky if consumer crashes mid-processing).
Manual ack: ack only after successful business logic (safer).


🧱 Durability (Interview Nuance)
For messages to survive broker restart, you typically need **both**:
1) Durable queue
2) Persistent message (`deliveryMode = 2`)

Durable exchange/queue alone is not enough if messages are transient.

Q: Does “persistent message” mean we save messages in our DB (MySQL/Postgres)?
A: No. RabbitMQ saves them on **its own disk**, not in your app database.

Simple meaning of each piece:

1) Durable queue
   Queue definition (name, bindings, settings) is saved on RabbitMQ disk.
   After broker restart → the queue still exists.

2) Persistent message (`deliveryMode = 2`)
   Message content is written to RabbitMQ disk.
   After broker restart → unacked / not-yet-consumed messages can come back.

   deliveryMode has only 2 standard values:
   | Value | Name                         | Meaning                                              |
   | ----- | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- |
   | **1** | Non-persistent (transient)   | Kept in memory (may be lost on broker restart)       |
   | **2** | Persistent                   | Written to RabbitMQ disk (can survive restart)       |

   So:
   - deliveryMode = 1 → faster, not safe across restart
   - deliveryMode = 2 → durable message body

   In Spring AMQP:
   - MessageDeliveryMode.NON_PERSISTENT → 1
   - MessageDeliveryMode.PERSISTENT → 2

   No other standard values are used for this flag.

3) Proper acks (manual ack)
   Consumer acks only after successful processing.
   If consumer crashes mid-work → message can be redelivered (not silently lost).

Quick table:

| Setup                                      | Survives broker restart?        |
| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------- |
| Durable queue + persistent message         | Yes (message stays in RabbitMQ) |
| Durable queue + transient message          | Queue yes, message often lost   |
| Non-durable queue                          | Queue + its messages gone       |

Interview one-liner:
Persist on RabbitMQ disk, not in your business database.
Need durable queue + persistent message (+ proper acks) to survive broker restart.


🛡️ Bonus Concepts
Dead Letter Queue (DLQ): Catches messages that can't be processed (rejected, expired, queue length exceeded).

Message TTL: Set message / queue expiration time.

Exclusive queue: Used by only one connection; deleted when that connection closes.

Auto-delete queue: Deleted when last consumer unsubscribes.


📦 RabbitMQ Setup in Java (Spring Boot)
1. Maven Dependency

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-amqp</artifactId>
</dependency>

2. Producer Example

@Autowired
private RabbitTemplate rabbitTemplate;

public void sendToQueue() {
    String message = "Order Created";
    rabbitTemplate.convertAndSend("my-exchange", "order.created", message);
}

3. Consumer Example

@RabbitListener(queues = "order-queue")
public void receive(String message) {
    System.out.println("Received: " + message);
}

4. Configuration Example

@Bean
public TopicExchange topicExchange() {
    return new TopicExchange("my-exchange");
}

@Bean
public Queue queue() {
    return new Queue("order-queue"); // durable by default in this constructor
}

@Bean
public Binding binding(Queue queue, TopicExchange exchange) {
    return BindingBuilder.bind(queue).to(exchange).with("order.*");
}


📊 RabbitMQ Use Cases
| Use Case                   | Example                                     |
| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Asynchronous processing    | User signup → send email in background      |
| Event-driven microservices | `OrderService` emits → others consume       |
| Pub/Sub                    | Fanout exchange → notify multiple systems   |
| Task queues / jobs         | OTP, SMS, OBD call workers                  |
| Retry & Dead Letter Queues | Retry failed events, log unprocessable ones |


🎯 Quick Interview Answers Cheatsheet
Q: Producer sends to queue directly?
A: Usually no — sends to **exchange**; exchange routes via bindings. (Default exchange is the exception.)

Q: Same queue, many consumers?
A: Competing consumers → load shared, one message → one consumer.

Q: How do all services get the same event?
A: Separate queues per service bound to Fanout/Topic (pub/sub).

Q: How to survive broker restart?
A: Durable queue + persistent messages (+ proper acks).
   Persist on RabbitMQ’s own disk (NOT your MySQL/Postgres).
   Durable queue = queue survives. Persistent msg = message body survives.

Q: RabbitMQ vs Kafka (1-liner)?
A: RabbitMQ = flexible routing + task/work queues; Kafka = durable high-throughput event log + replay.
